Header Only Version
==================================================================
Just copy the files to your build tree and use a C++11 compiler.  
Or use CMake:
``` 
  add_executable(example_header_only example.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(example_header_only spdlog::spdlog_header_only)
```

Compiled Library Version
==================================================================
CMake:
```  
  add_executable(example example.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(example spdlog::spdlog)
```

Or copy files src/*.cpp to your build tree and pass the -DSPDLOG_COMPILED_LIB to the compiler.

C++20 Module Version (experimental)
==================================================================
Build spdlog with -DSPDLOG_BUILD_MODULE=ON, then:
```
  find_package(spdlog CONFIG REQUIRED COMPONENTS module)
  add_executable(example_module module_example.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(example_module spdlog::spdlog_module)
  set_target_properties(example_module PROPERTIES CXX_SCAN_FOR_MODULES ON)
```

Requirements: CMake 3.28 or newer, the Ninja (1.11+) or Visual Studio 2026 generator, and
gcc 15+, clang 17+ or MSVC 19.50+ (Visual Studio 2026). Configuring with SPDLOG_BUILD_MODULE=ON on anything older
fails with an explanation rather than a confusing build error.

Things worth knowing:

* Binary module interfaces are not distributable. What gets installed is the module source,
  <prefix>/share/spdlog/module/spdlog.cppm, and it is recompiled inside your build. Your
  compiler therefore does not have to match the one that built libspdlog, but your C++
  standard and your SPDLOG_* configuration do - exactly as with the headers.
* The module links the compiled library. There is no header-only module.
* `import spdlog;` gives you spdlog's names only. Standard library names (std::shared_ptr,
  std::string, ...) still come from the usual #includes, and a module cannot export macros,
  so the SPDLOG_INFO(..) family lives in <spdlog/macros.h>.
* On MSVC, include your standard headers *before* `import spdlog;`. Including one afterwards
  produces a cascade of C2572 "redefinition of default argument" errors inside the MSVC
  standard library. Clang and gcc accept either order.
* On MSVC, also #include <spdlog/mdc.h> if you use spdlog::mdc: MSVC cannot emit the
  thread_local inside mdc::get_context() from an imported declaration and fails to link with
  "unresolved external symbol __tlregdtor". The include is harmless on other compilers.
* Mixing `import spdlog;` with `import std;` in the same translation unit is not supported.
  Mixing `import spdlog;` with #include <spdlog/...> across translation units of one program
  is fully supported - that is the point of the wrapper-module design.
* No pkg-config file is generated for the module; modules cannot be expressed in pkg-config.
* If you override SPDLOG_NAMESPACE or SPDLOG_NAMESPACE_BEGIN/END, pass the same flags to the
  build that compiles spdlog.cppm.
* With CMake older than 4.4, keep the consuming target's CXX_STANDARD equal to the one spdlog
  was built with; per-consumer module compilation only arrived in CMake 4.4.

Important Information for Compilation:
==================================================================
* If you encounter compilation errors with gcc 4.8.x, please note that gcc 4.8.x does not fully support C++11. In such cases, consider upgrading your compiler or using a different version that fully supports C++11 standards

Tested on:  
gcc 4.8.1 and above
clang 3.5
Visual Studio 2013